


	           Comparison of ADA Environment Requirements
	              with UCI Lisp Environment Facilities


	                        Stephen Willson
	                        January 21, 1981

	       This informal paper first compares the ADA  environment
	  requirements  mentioned in class with the features available
	  in  UCI  Lisp,  and  then  discusses  how  those   currently
	  available  features  could  help us to implement the desired
	  features for an ADA environment, and the impact of  such  an
	  approach on the design of the ADA interpreter.

	       1.  Run a program or execute an expression at  the  top
	           level.  This facility exists.

	       2.  Suspend a  running  program  (^C).   This  facility
	           exists.

	       3.  Indicate where suspended.   This  facility  exists,
	           though  not  exactly  in  as  pleasant a form as is
	           desired.

	       4.  At point of suspension:

	           1.  Look at variables.  This facility exists.

	           2.  Perform assignments.  This facility exists.

	           3.  Call a subprogram.  This facility exists.

	           4.  Resume execution.  This facility exists.

	           5.  Return a value from  a  function  (exiting  the
	               function).  This facility exists.

	           6.  Transfer control via GOTO.  This  facility  not
	               available.

	           7.  Exit a loop.  This facility not available.


	       5.  Call [structure] Editor to edit  a  granule.   This
	           facility exists.

	       6.  Allow  breakpoints  and  tracing.   This   facility
	           exists.    "Freeze  universe  on  condition."  This
	           facility not available.

	       7.  Call file package (input/output/directories).  This
	           facility exists.
	                                                        Page 2


	       8.  Single Step.  This facility not readily  available.

	       9.  Sending messages.  This facility not available.

	      10.  Getting help.  This facility not available.


	       Possiblities for using  current  UCI  Lisp  environment
	  capablities to create ADA environment desired:

	       1.  If we alter the (print (eval (read))) loop  to  use
	           our  version of read always, then at any point that
	           the user may wish to enter  an  ADA  form  he  may,
	           without  having  to worry about messing up the Lisp
	           scanner.  We can also enforce this use of our  read
	           routine  by setting initfn to be our own evaluation
	           loop.  This means that if a disaster happens  while
	           an  ADA user is using the ADA environment, he won't
	           suddenly wind up in  the  Lisp  environment.   This
	           solves  the  "((worker))"  problem  as mentioned in
	           class (Eliza).

	       2.  If we binding mechanism similar to  Lisp's,  namely
	           by  keeping  all  visible  objects and functions on
	           visible property lists and all hidden  objects  and
	           functions stored in a dynamic stack, then (perhaps)
	           the work of implementing the functionality  desired
	           during breaks can be simplified.

	           Lisp has hooks for allowing the break package to be
	           called.   If  our  interpreter is rolling along and
	           wants to handle a break, then our interpreter could
	           call  the  Lisp  break  package.   This package has
	           hooks for allowing us to enter  our  own  functions
	           that  can  modify  the  run-time stack and variable
	           bindings in the ADA  interpreter.   This,  combined
	           with  the  use  of  our  own  read  routine,  would
	           hopefully  allow  us  to  implement  an  ADA  break
	           package  that  would  give  us the added benefit of
	           being potentially able to execute  the  Lisp  break
	           package,  thus  facilitating  the  debugging of our
	           system.

	       3.  If desired, a facility for getting help and sending
	           messages  could  be  added  by adding some macro-10
	           code to manipulate a pseudo-tty.  Suppose I want to
	           send  a message.  I could type ^C^B, enter the Lisp
	           break package that  had  been  augmented  with  our
	           special  features,  send  the  message  via  a  pty
	           without harming my current  environment,  and  then
	           continue  execution  of  my  ADA program.  The same
	           scheme could likewise be used to get help.
	                                                        Page 3


	       4.  Single stepping could simply be an  option  to  the
	           ADA interpreter.

	       5.  Calls to the file package  would  be  the  same  as
	           being   able  to  execute  an  arbitrary  (visible)
	           function during a break.  If we use  our  own  read
	           routine,  then the user could simply enter the call
	           and have it  executed  immediately.   Likewise  for
	           calling  the  Editor.  The Editor would not require
	           extensive knowledge of the internal structures used
	           by  the  interpreter  if  the visible functions are
	           stored on property lists.


	       Summary:

	  By writing the ADA interpreter in such a way that it uses 

	       1.  property lists to store  the  visible  objects  and
	           functions,

	       2.  has hooks  available  via  the  break  package  for
	           manipulation during breaks,

	       3.  and enforcing the use of our own read routine


	  it will be possible to use the features already  present  in
	  Lisp to

	       1.  debug our interpreter,

	       2.  allow addition of "neat" environment features  like
	           messages and help,

	       3.  give  us  a  flexible  framework  for  adding   new
	           features (via break macros),

	       4.  and allow us a  great  deal  of  control  over  the
	           characters   the  user  might  type  (including  ^C
	           interrupts).

